<p>Senior US and Iranian delegations arrived in Islamabad on Saturday for high-stakes talks aimed at turning a fragile 14-day ceasefire into a broader deal after more than six weeks of war involving the United States, Iran and Israel. Vice President JD Vance is leading the US team, while Iran’s delegation is led by Parliament Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf and includes Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi. Pakistan is hosting the meeting after helping broker the current pause in fighting. The talks offer a real opening for diplomacy, but they begin under visible strain. Multiple outlets report that Iran tied its participation to a ceasefire in Lebanon and the release of frozen Iranian assets, while President Trump and Vance paired public optimism with warnings that the truce could collapse if no deal emerges. A durable agreement would matter far beyond the negotiating room: the war has killed thousands across the Middle East, disrupted energy supplies, and left the Strait of Hormuz not yet fully reopened, raising the stakes for regional stability and the global economy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GtiGA7A5vsAyCTzfE3v658twgD2oNSmzSLKVCZ-ItUUdaotu5R_q-wOIqnMzoSdLh-2pcEbpoN-JhCDsH6zhpBB2O1x4gTohD6m1cuydNUAv' alt='US Vice President JD Vance disembarks from Air Force Two after arriving for talks with Iranian officials in Islamabad on April 11, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Large delegation: The Iranian team includes at least 70 members. The New York Times said the scale was meant to project unity and show that Tehran sees the talks as broader than a narrow political exchange.</li><li>First direct: Dawn described the Islamabad meeting as the first direct high-level engagement between Washington and Tehran since 1979, underscoring how unusual the encounter is even before any agreement is reached.</li><li>Five disputes: The BBC identified five main sticking points: Iran’s nuclear programme, the Strait of Hormuz, sanctions and frozen assets, the scope of any ceasefire, and the role Israel and Lebanon would play in a final settlement.</li><li>Pakistan role: Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif called the negotiations “make or break,” reflecting Islamabad’s effort to turn its ceasefire mediation into a longer-term diplomatic track.</li></ul><blockquote>We’ll see how it turns out. So it’s JD and Steve and Jared. We have a good team, and they meet tomorrow. We’ll see how it all works out. - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: Vice President Vance said he expected the talks to be positive but warned Iran not to “try to play us,” showing that Washington is entering the meeting with guarded expectations, not trust. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/5a14a4dd-0042-46bf-bbdf-bc0cc19635b1'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Iran: Parliament Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf said negotiations should not begin until there is a ceasefire in Lebanon and Iranian assets blocked abroad are released, framing Tehran’s position as conditional rather than open-ended. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/04/10/world/iran-war-trump-israel-lebanon/a-senior-iranian-official-says-blocked-assets-must-be-released-before-talks-with-the-us-can-begin'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Pakistan’s Foreign Office said Deputy Prime Minister Ishaq Dar urged the parties to engage constructively and repeated Islamabad’s desire to keep facilitating a lasting, durable solution. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990461/vance-en-route-iranian-delegation-touches-down-for-islamabad-talks'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Israel: Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said there was no ceasefire in Lebanon, highlighting a key disagreement over whether the Lebanon front is part of the broader truce framework. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/apr/10/iran-war-live-updates-trump-ceasefire-strait-hormuz-israel-lebanon-hezbollah'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>United Kingdom: Prime Minister Keir Starmer described the ceasefire as fragile, reflecting broader international concern that regional fighting could still derail the negotiations. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/10/jd-vance-warns-iran-not-to-play-us-during-planned-talks-in-pakistan'>Euronews</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/5a14a4dd-0042-46bf-bbdf-bc0cc19635b1'>JD Vance arrives in Pakistan for ‘positive’ peace talks with Iran</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-iran-set-for-high-stakes-talks-in-pakistan/live-76745065'>US, Iran set for high-stakes talks in Pakistan</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/politics/article/3349714/us-presses-iran-talks-gear-pakistan-under-fragile-ceasefire'>US presses Iran as talks gear up in Pakistan under fragile ceasefire</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2wyn8wdz0o'>The five big sticking points in US-Iran talks</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/04/10/world/iran-war-trump-israel-lebanon'>Iranian Delegation Arrives at Peace Talks, With Vance en Route</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990743/us-vice-president-jd-vance-arrives-in-islamabad-for-make-or-break-talks-with-iran'>US Vice President JD Vance arrives in Islamabad for ‘make-or-break’ talks with Iran</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/apr/10/iran-war-live-updates-trump-ceasefire-strait-hormuz-israel-lebanon-hezbollah'>Trump warns of fresh strikes if Iran talks fail – as it happened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/11/pakistans-prime-minister-calls-us-iran-talks-make-or'>Pakistan’s prime minister calls US-Iran talks ‘make or break’</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/iran-set-for-peace-talks-but-doubts-emerge-over-lebanon/'>Iran set for peace talks but doubts emerge over Lebanon</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://